      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "6ae35e50f427a4ecc8e30f28",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": {
        "like": 2
      },
      "restoredAt": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Blocking also enables targeted harassment. It lets users single out a specific person and permanently exclude them from their experience. Friends can encourage each other to block the same person, leaving that person unable to participate in conversations with people who have decided they don't want to hear from them. The blocked user has no way to appeal that decision or require anyone to reconsider. Someone could block them once and never see their content again, even after they've changed. If that's a reason to reject content filters, it should apply to people too. Removing blocking would prevent this kind of exclusion and give every user a fair chance to keep explaining themselves until the other person agrees to interact with them.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
    {
      "_id": "6ae65be3df81fcf66c3feaf0",
      "aiGenerated": false,
      "author": {
        "_id": "5f2d710dc1c881463f153789",
        "alias": "Remarkable Grasshopper",
        "aliasID": "65c4ab08ed6eefce4c1d7d7b",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": "https://canny-assets.io/images/bc2f9f6d7202a7992d3e3ad7f6bfdf27.png",
        "created": "2020-08-07T15:19:41.359Z",
        "name": "Fusl",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": null
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "58c62efae9868f3662e9d9a1",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2026-10-31T21:21:39.608Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "deletedAt": null,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": "6ae65adfdf81fcf66c3fcd24",
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "6ae35e50f427a4ecc8e30f28",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "restoredAt": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Wait, hold up, I gotta appeal to the slop-lovers. Here you go:\n\nI support this request. Blocking gives users a tool to single out another person and exclude them from their experience based entirely on their own judgment. There is no requirement to establish that the person violated a rule. Someone can decide they don't like what another user says or creates, press a button, and stop seeing them. If allowing people to filter out content enables targeted harassment against its creators, allowing them to filter out the creators themselves deserves at least the same scrutiny.\n\nThe potential for coordinated exclusion is clear. A user can block someone and then tell their friends why. Those friends might make the same decision. The result is several people refusing to interact with a particular person, without that person ever agreeing to the arrangement. Some of those people might even trust their friends' accounts instead of personally experiencing the behavior first. A platform concerned about targeted exclusion should question whether users need tools that make this possible.\n\nCalling it a personal preference doesn't resolve the issue. Every block targets a specific account. The person who blocks someone may describe it as curating their own experience, but the blocked person experiences a loss of access to an audience. We shouldn't accept one description over the other simply because we believe people should control who gets to speak to them.\n\nThere's also no meaningful opportunity for the blocked user to appeal. They cannot require the person to explain the decision or hear their side of the story. They might have a perfectly good explanation for whatever prompted the block, yet the feature allows the other person to avoid hearing it. It seems unfair to let someone decide that a conversation is over while another participant still has things they want to say.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
    {
      "_id": "6ae65be75db39a115772c89b",
      "aiGenerated": false,
      "author": {
        "_id": "5f2d710dc1c881463f153789",
        "alias": "Remarkable Grasshopper",
        "aliasID": "65c4ab08ed6eefce4c1d7d7b",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": "https://canny-assets.io/images/bc2f9f6d7202a7992d3e3ad7f6bfdf27.png",
        "created": "2020-08-07T15:19:41.359Z",
        "name": "Fusl",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": null
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "58c62efae9868f3662e9d9a1",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2026-10-31T21:21:43.307Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "deletedAt": null,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": "6ae65adfdf81fcf66c3fcd24",
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "6ae35e50f427a4ecc8e30f28",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "restoredAt": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Blocks can also outlast the circumstances that caused them. Someone might change their behavior or adopt a different creative process. The person who blocked them might never find out, because the block continues doing exactly what they asked it to do. This deprives the blocked user of future opportunities to regain their attention. At minimum, anyone who chooses to block another user should have to periodically review that person's activity to determine whether continued nonparticipation is justified.\n\nThe usual argument is that blocking doesn't stop anyone from using the platform. It only stops them from interacting with a particular person. But the same argument applies to content filters. Filtering something out of your own view doesn't remove it from the platform or prevent other people from enjoying it. If that distinction isn't sufficient to defend a content filter, it shouldn't suddenly become sufficient when defending a block button.\n\nLikewise, saying that blocking protects users from harassment assumes we can distinguish between avoiding unwanted contact and unfairly denying someone contact they want. The block button leaves that decision to the person receiving the attention. The person seeking their attention gets no vote at all. That's a substantial amount of authority to give someone over their own social experience.\n\nRemoving blocking would address this inconsistency. Users could still choose not to engage, but they would have to keep making that choice manually whenever the other person approached them. This would preserve the opportunity for dialogue instead of allowing one participant to end it permanently through a settings menu.\n\nIf a creator's access to an unwilling audience takes priority over that audience's filtering preferences, the same principle should apply to individual users. Everyone should have an equal opportunity to continue explaining why you should want to interact with them, regardless of how often you've already decided otherwise.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
